1. Write some pseudocode to calculate and output the product of two numbers input
by the user. Recall the words commonly used when writing pseudocode such as
INPUT, COMPUTE, and OUTPUT.

START
OUTPUT "What is your first number?"
INPUT number1
OUTPUT "What is your second number?"
INPUT number2
COMPUTE product <- number1 + number2
OUTPUT product
END


2. Write some pseudocode to calculate and output both the quotient (the result of
   dividing one number by another) and the remainder of two numbers input by the
   user.

START
OUTPUT What is your first number?
INPUT Number1
OUTPUT What is your second number?
INPUT Number2
COMPUTE quotient <- Number1 // Number2
COMPUTE remainder <-Number1 MOD Number2
OUTPUT "The quotient is:" + quotient
Output "The remainder is:" + remainder
END

3. Write some pseudocode to output a yes/no value in response to a young person's
   request for driving lessons. Think about what data you will need to generate a
   result. Note that the output need not be yes/no. A Boolean value, e.g. true/false
   will suffice.

  START
  OUTPUT "How old are you?"
  INPUT Age
  IF age >= 17
    OUTPUT "You are eligible for driving lessons. Do you have a provisional licence?"
  INPUT ProvisionalLicence
  IF ProvisionalLicence = TRUE
    OUTPUT "You can book lessons"
  ELSE
    OUTPUT "You cannot book lessons sorry"
  ENDIF
  ELSE
    OUTPUT "You are not old enough, sorry"
  ENDIF
  END

  -------------------
  IF age >= 17 THEN
    OUTPUT true
  ELSE
    OUTPUT false
  END IF


